        "title": "3.3 Intermediary Coordination Function",
        "overview": "",
        "whatItIs": "An intermediary coordination function is a dedicated, trusted unit that provides day-to-day intermediary coordination between governments, programs and institutions working together towards shared climate goals. In doing so, the intermediary coordination helps develop shared capability to connect institutions, interpret implementation experience and strengthen the wider multilevel governance process. It can sit within a national platform, government programme or other trusted institution and typically combines analysis, facilitation, policy alignment, monitoring and learning. The function does not replace the formal mandates or decision-making authority of participating institutions; instead, it helps them work across institutional and governance boundaries.",
        "whyItIsNeeded": "The implementation of climate projects often spans siloed institutions with different mandates, resources and decision cycles. To this end, intermediary coordination functions are needed to avoid siloes, align mandates and help sustain coordination beyond specific projects, programs or funding cycles. They also help create institutional memory by through by enabling recurring coordination, documentation of processes and feedback loops, enabling strong institutional relationships, accountability and progressively improving multilevel climate governance system.",
