        "title": "4.1 Joint Climate Investment Planning and Budgeting",
        "overview": "",
        "whatItIs": "Investment planning and budgeting are complementary activities that provide a recurring process through which national, subnational and city governments can translate shared climate goals into investment priorities, sequencing, responsibilities and indicative resource requirements. Joint investment planning helps identify which climate investments are required, who is expected to deliver and finance them, when they should occur, and how funding gaps and risks will be addressed. Once the investments are identified and planned, climate budgeting then involves integrating priority projects into operating and capital budgets of a municipality or higher levels of government, assigning implementation responsibilities and tracking delivery through the regular budget cycles. When undertaken jointly by national, subnational and local governments, these activities can help align local investment needs with national policies, funding programmes and fiscal decisions – strengthening coordinated climate action across levels of government.",
        "whyItIsNeeded": "Climate action plans may often set out actions without a clear understanding of their costs, financing responsibilities or links to regular budget decisions, which may contribute to the planning-to-investment gap faced by climate projects. Joint climate investment planning and budgeting can therefore help align priorities across levels of government, identify financing needs and gaps and embed agreed investments into routine operating and capital budgets so that climate commitments are translated into coordinated implementation.",
