        "title": "4.2 Portfolio and Pipeline Development",
        "overview": "",
        "whatItIs": "Portfolio and pipeline development involves translating agreed climate priorities into a coordinated portfolio of interventions and progressively identifying and preparing the investible projects that emerge from it. A portfolio can package projects with enabling policy, governance, procurement, capacity and implementation measures around a common mission, through coordination and aggregation across city, regional and national levels. While not every portfolio intervention needs to be investible from the outset, portfolios can generate a pipeline of projects and investment opportunities that can then be prepared for financing. Therefore, portfolios represent a promising approach to crowd in finance and move from investment planning toward implementation.",
        "whyItIsNeeded": "A project-by-project approach is often insufficient to scale climate investment and implementation. Countries and cities need a portfolio of interventions, with a pipeline of well-prepared projects, integrated with enabling regulations, governance functions, procurement, financing and implementation pathways. This can help turn individual initiatives into a well-connected, sequenced and bankable package of interventions. This way, a portfolio approach could make the path from concepts to implementation clearer and more visible for financiers and investors.",
        "keyActors": [
          "National Government",
          "Subnational Government",
          "Technical Assistance Providers",
          "Infrastructure Developers",
          "Utilities",
          "Private Investors",
          "Development Finance Institutions",
          "Civil Society"
        ],
