        "title": "Locally Anchored Multilevel Climate Finance Structures",
        "overview": "",
        "whatItIs": "Locally anchored multilevel climate finance structures are governance and investment arrangements that help translate local climate portfolios into prepared investments and credible financing pathways. They can combine local ownership and portfolio governance with project preparation, capital matching and coordination with national and external finance. Their purpose is to create a local mechanism for cities to mobilize and deploy climate finance from diverse sources, including national governments, donors or private financiers. Their institutional and financial form will vary by context and may use existing municipal balance sheets, public banks, trust funds, SPVs, pooled borrowing, bonds or other financing vehicles.",
        "whyItIsNeeded": "Climate portfolios can contain investments involving different owners, maturity levels, risks and financing needs. The challenge is therefore not only mobilizing capital but organizing the portfolio so that suitable components can be prepared and matched with appropriate sources of finance, while retaining local ownership and alignment with the wider transition. These finance vehicles and mechanisms can help bridge this gap by connecting portfolios with available capital and can take different forms depending on local government capacity, governance and market conditions.",
        "keyActors": [
          "National Government",
          "Subnational Government",
          "National Development Banks",
          "Commercial Banks",
          "Private Investors",
          "Special Purpose Vehicles",
          "Philanthropic Actors",
          "Community Stakeholders"
        ],
        "transferability": [
          "Tailor the financing structure to the local fiscal system, borrowing regulations, tax base and market maturity: where decentralization and private-sector depth are stronger, local trust funds or similar vehicles may work well; where responsibilities are devolved but fiscal space is limited, devolved funds may be a better fit; and in more unitary systems, country- or donor-led funds, like the Viability Fund or LoCAL program, may need to be channelled through existing fiscal transfer mechanisms. In context where local governments have creditworthiness, a green bond framework like Lund could be utilized.",
          "When setting up a climate investment fund, start with a focused investment portfolio, aligned with national priorities and plans, so that investor confidence improves and external finance could be mobilized.",
          "Carefully structure SPVs for project financing and implementation. SPVs are legally complex and expensive downscaling to smaller projects requires careful packaging and de-risking for banks and commercial investors to participate.",
          "Regardless of where the fund gets anchored, enable local planning control while bringing in external capital through multilevel partnerships, so finance supports locally defined and locally led climate priorities."
        ],
