            "title": "LoCAL",
            "type": "Illustrative Example",
            "connectionLine": "Why see this: See how climate finance can be channelled to local governments through existing national fiscal-transfer and planning systems.",
            "excerpt": "LoCAL is a country-based mechanism spearheaded by the UN Capital Development Fund (UNCDF) that helps local governments integrate climate adaptation into planning and budgeting.",
            "fullText": "LoCAL is a country-based mechanism spearheaded by the UN Capital Development Fund (UNCDF) It helps local governments integrate climate adaptation into planning and budgeting through performance-based climate resilience grants and technical and capacity-building support. It channels climate finance to local governments through existing national fiscal transfer systems. In practice, local governments assess risks and needs, prepare adaptation plans, identify and budget priority measures, and implement them through their annual budgeting cycles; performance reviews and audits then inform future allocations. LoCAL is designed to scale in phases, moving from piloting to learning and then to full national roll-out, which makes it a strong example of a multilevel local climate finance structure embedded in national systems. Since 2011, LoCAL has provided USD 8.3 million in grants to 64 local governments, impacting over 5 million people across 12 countries in Asia (Bangladesh, Bhutan, Cambodia, Lao PDR and Nepal), the Pacific (Tuvalu) and Africa (Benin, Ghana, Mali, Mozambique, Niger and Tanzania).",
