            "title": "Urban Infrastructure Insurance Facility (UIIF)",
            "type": "Illustrative Example",
            "connectionLine": "Why see this: See how risk assessment, insurance and pooling can help address climate and disaster risks affecting urban infrastructure.",
            "excerpt": "Urban Infrastructure Insurance Facility (UIIF) is a multilevel insurance-based risk pooling mechanism involving municipal governments, international donor agencies and city networks.",
            "fullText": "Urban Infrastructure Insurance Facility (UIIF) is a multilevel insurance-based risk pooling mechanism involving municipal governments, international donor agencies and city networks. It demonstrates how cities can address climate and disaster exposure risks and protect critical infrastructure. In the UIIF model, ten cities in Latin America and the Caribbean undergo risk assessments that inform tailored insurance coverage and disaster risk reduction measures, with individual city risks transferred into a pooled structure that reduces premiums through economies of scale and risk sharing. After a disaster, cities can access financing more quickly to rebuild infrastructure and support vulnerable residents, while also benefiting from better risk management, legal and technical support, and stronger relationships with financial institutions. UIIF follows a 7-stage roadmap, which includes profiling and selecting cities, assessing risk and tailoring products, designing premium subsidies, tendering the insurance solution, and disbursing the subsidy over time.",
            "link": "https://citiesclimatefinance.org/resources/urban-infrastructure-insurance-facility/"
