        "title": "Aggregated Procurement as a Market-Shaping and Industrial Transition Mechanism",
        "overview": "",
        "whatItIs": "Aggregated and pooled procurement are a market-shaping and industrial transition mechanisms in which cities, alongside subnational and national agencies and other public and private buyers coordinate purchasing power to create a stronger, aggregated demand for low-carbon and resilience-enhancing goods, services and infrastructure. The market signal can be strengthened further if local procurement activities are aligned with national climate priorities. In this pathway, aggregation can mean multiple buyers coordinating demand while keeping separate budgets and contracts, while pooled procurement combines demand into a single fund or joint tender. The mechanism can operate within a city, across municipalities, or vertically across city, state and national levels.",
        "whyItIsNeeded": "Subnational governments collectively spend over USD 6 trillion a year on goods and services – roughly 8% of global GDP – yet this leverage is fragmented across departments and not coordinated with private demand, so markets receive no stable signal to invest in low-carbon materials. Procurement, when used strategically to align with climate outcomes, can help create a unified, more stable demand for green and resilient infrastructure.",
